#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The Claude Code session picker (prefix+C-c) and the one command-line entry
to the conversation full-text index.

Default view: the running sessions in the ctrl+alt+c usage panel's columns
(Enter jumps to the pane). `/fts word` searches the text of live AND archived
conversations together (live rows keep their columns, archived ones leave the
live-only cells blank; Enter resumes an archived one in a new tmux window under
its own account); `/fts/live` and `/fts/archive` restrict it to one side
(`/fts/archive` also switches to the shorter past-session columns). Full text is the Recoll index kept by
claude-fts-index (see fts_search); `--fts`, `--sessions`, `--snippets` and
`--resume-window` are the entry points alt-tab and the usage panel call.

Same query language idea as this dotfiles repo's tmux
window-search.py (prefix+C-w over live scrollback), but this tool is not a
tmux script: it's a plain CLI, run directly (`claude-history`) in any
terminal. fzf's own default full-screen mode already takes over the
terminal via the alternate screen buffer and restores it on exit - the same
"popup" feel window-search.py got from tmux's display-popup, but for free
and without a tmux dependency. The corpus is every past conversation Claude
Code has ever saved, in ~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl (one file per session;
nested */subagents/agent-*.jsonl files are forked subagent transcripts, not
resumable top-level sessions, so the glob below deliberately stays at
exactly one directory level and never recurses into them).

One path through Enter (resolve()): a live session -> switch-client there
and close the pane this was invoked from, since it was just a scratch
launcher for the search, fair game once its job is done (see
close_origin_pane). Not live -> a new tmux window running `claude --resume
<id> --dangerously-skip-permissions` under the account it last ran as
(resume_tmux); outside tmux, exec in place of this process.

prefix+C-c (.tmux.conf) runs this in a brand-new window
(`new-window ... claude-history`), not via send-keys into the current pane
(a prior version did that, and broke: that pane could be running anything
- another Claude Code session reading raw keystrokes, vim, whatever - not
necessarily a shell prompt ready to accept a typed command, so injecting
text into its stdin landed as garbage input to whatever was actually
running there) and not via a tmux popup either (tried next: fixed the
send-keys problem, but broke two other things, confirmed by testing both
directly - display-popup computes its size once at creation and never
follows the client being resized afterward, unlike a real pane, which
resizes live like any other tmux pane; and a popup's environment has
$TMUX but never $TMUX_PANE, which silently broke the live-session location
column since in_tmux() requires both). A real new window sidesteps every
one of those: it's a normal pane (resizes live, sets $TMUX_PANE normally),
and it never touches whatever pane was actually focused, so the exact
same close-the-launcher-pane behavior as the plain CLI is correct here
too - no separate popup-mode path needed at all.

tmux integration itself is opportunistic, detected at runtime from $TMUX/
$TMUX_PANE (both set by tmux for every real pane - no bind-key plumbing
needed to learn "which pane am I"):
  - outside tmux entirely: skip the live/switch dance altogether and just
    exec-resume - there is no meaningful "switch to another pane" without
    tmux, so integrating with it "only if available" means not pretending
    to when it isn't.
  - inside tmux: "live right now" is answered by Claude Code's own
    bookkeeping rather than guessed. Every running interactive session
    maintains ~/.claude*/sessions/<pid>.json (one per CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR -
    .claude, .claude2, .claude3 here all happen to share the same
    underlying ~/.claude/projects via symlink, but each keeps its own
    independent sessions/ directory of currently-running pids) containing
    sessionId, pid, procStart (the process's /proc/<pid>/stat start-time,
    in clock ticks - the same disambiguator the kernel itself uses to tell
    a live pid from a reused one), and, when launched inside tmux, a "tmux"
    field of the form "session_name:@window_id.%pane_id" - a pane_id is a
    stable, globally unique tmux target on its own, no window/session
    lookup needed. This was found by inspecting that directory directly
    (see the window-search-debugging-session memory for the same "verify
    against real state" approach) rather than inferring liveness from
    process argv or scanning /proc/*/fd for an open transcript file - the
    latter looked appealing but Claude Code opens, appends, and closes the
    jsonl per write rather than holding it open, so an fd-scan mostly
    observes "not open" for a session that is very much running. procStart
    is re-checked against /proc/<pid>/stat at both list-time and jump-time,
    and the claimed pane_id is checked against this tmux server's own
    `list-panes -a` before ever attempting a switch - a sessions/*.json
    file left behind by a killed/crashed process is a stale claim not a
    live one, pid reuse by the OS would otherwise make a stale entry look
    alive again, and the live session might belong to a different tmux
    server (a different -L socket) than the one this process is inside.
    Live-but-unreachable-from-here still refuses to resume (to avoid a
    second writer on the same transcript) even though it can't switch to
    it either.

Tab completes the query DSL (see the "query language" comment below and
complete_query) - a unique field/value completes directly, an ambiguous
one opens a small nested fzf popup to pick from.

Startup is "fzf first, data second": fzf is launched before any history
is read, the cached index is loaded on a thread (~0.1s) and served to it
from memory over a unix socket (see PickerServer), and whatever changed
on disk since the cache was written is reparsed by a niced, detached
child that streams sessions back one at a time while an "indexing N%"
border label counts up - typing and picking work throughout. Before this
the same picker took 2-3s to become responsive: every keystroke was a
fresh python process re-reading a 16MB snapshot, and the reindex ran
un-niced against it.

Deps: fzf, python3. tmux is optional - only needed for the live/switch path.
"""
import argparse
import os as _os, sys as _sys
_sys.path.insert(0, _os.path.expanduser("~/.config/dsl"))
import dsl  # shared DSL primitives (~/.config/dsl/dsl.py)
import atexit
import glob
import http.client
import json
import os
import pickle
import re
import base64
import shlex
import signal
import socket
import socketserver
import struct
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import threading
import time
from concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor, as_completed
from collections import namedtuple

CLAUDE_DIR = os.path.expanduser(os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR", "~/.claude"))
PROJECTS_GLOB = os.path.join(CLAUDE_DIR, "projects", "*", "*.jsonl")  # (message text only)


def project_files():
    """Every transcript of every ~/.claude* profile, each distinct projects/
    directory once (most profiles symlink to ~/.claude/projects, so a plain
    union of globs would list every file seven times). One corpus for all
    profiles = one index/cache and the same results from any pane."""
    seen, files = set(), []
    for d in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.expanduser("~/.claude*/projects"))):
        real = os.path.realpath(d)
        if real in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(real)
        files += glob.glob(os.path.join(real, "*", "*.jsonl"))
    return files
# every CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR-like directory keeps its own live-session
# registry (see module docstring) - bounded to ~/.claude* (a handful of
# dirs), never an unbounded $HOME scan
SESSIONS_GLOB = os.path.expanduser("~/.claude*/sessions/*.json")

MAX_MSG_CHARS = 20000  # cap one message's indexed/stored text - same guard
# window-search's CAPTURE_LINES is, against one pasted file dump or huge
# tool transcript blowing up snapshot size/tokenize time for one outlier
TOKEN_RE = re.compile(r"[a-z0-9]+")

_DEBUG = os.path.exists(os.path.expanduser("~/.cache/claude-history-search-debug"))
_T0 = time.time()


def _dbg(msg):
    if not _DEBUG:
        return
    with open(os.path.expanduser("~/.cache/claude-history-search-timing.log"), "a") as f:
        f.write(f"[abs={time.time():.3f} rel={time.time() - _T0:.3f} pid={os.getpid()}] {msg}\n")


def tokenize(text):
    return TOKEN_RE.findall(text.lower())


# --- query language: identical to window-search.py's base language (see
# there for the full reasoning) - bare words are prefix-expanded and ANDed,
# "phrases" are exact and required, !negated drops, and a bare word
# carrying punctuation ("--stat", "ctrl+.") gets a soft literal bonus
# without filtering anything. Duplicated rather than imported: these two
# tools evolve independently and the query engine is the only thing they
# share (same reasoning focus-picker.py gives for not sharing jump() as a
# module).
#
# On top of that base language, this tool adds a field-scoped term:
# `/fv/field word` (also `/filter-value/field`) restricts word to one
# field instead of the whole document - the field is glued onto the verb
# with a second `/` (the "via" operator, see ~/.config/docs/query-dsl.md's
# "Via paths"), no colon involved at all: `/fv/tit foo` searches only
# title, `/fv/x foo` searches only tmux (field is substring-resolved - any
# FIELD_NAMES entry whose name *contains* it, unioned - not just a prefix).
# The verb slot may be left empty: `//field word` is `/fv/field word`
# (query-dsl.md "Default verb" - /fv is the default verb when a via "/" is
# used with nothing before it).
# `/fv/field` alone (no word following) is a no-op - there's no group/
# subfield distinction here (every FIELD_NAMES entry is already flat), so
# unlike a bare group path elsewhere in this DSL there's no existence
# check to fall back to either (query-dsl.md "Via paths": a flat-type via
# path with nothing following it yet contributes nothing, rather than
# matching or excluding anything). This has to be an explicit no-op, not
# just "an empty term that happens to always match" - the negated form,
# `!/fv/field` alone, would otherwise silently exclude every session
# instead of contributing nothing (reported 2026-09-02 for the sibling
# QML/Rust implementations' free-text-fallback version of this same bug;
# this tool's own failure mode was different but just as real). `!/fv/
# ...` negates. Keys are a comma list, fuzzy (exact name wins, else
# substring); an unresolvable key list (`/fv/qqq foo`, or still being typed)
# degrades to a plain bare-word search over the literal text instead of
# being dropped. The colon form (`/fv field:word`) is gone. The other
# shared-grammar verbs (/ft, /at, /rt, /s) are recognised but inert
# here: fixed columns. /s[/keys[/dir]] orders the rows (same keys as the usage panel; /s/age = newest first). Full text is `/fts word` (Recoll),
# only live ones `/fts/live`, only past ones `/fts/archive`.
#
#   path     session's working directory
#   tmux     session:window.pane, if currently live and reachable here
#   session  just the session name piece of that
#   window   just the window index piece
#   pane     just the pane index piece
#   title    the AI-generated title
#   time     the displayed relative age ("14d ago", "45m ago") - what's
#            actually shown in the TIME column, not a raw timestamp
#   body     the actual message content (title excluded - that's `title`)
#
# path/tmux/session/window/pane/time are short structured strings, not
# prose - `//path conf` is a plain case-insensitive substring test against the
# whole field, not tokenized/ranked. body is the conversation text, searched
# through the Recoll index like /fts.
FIELD_NAMES = ["path", "tmux", "session", "window", "pane", "title", "time", "status", "acct", "body"]
# /s keys: the usage panel's names (ClaudeUsageExpanded searchTypeNames), so /s/age sorts alike in both
SORT_FIELDS = ["account", "status", "title", "msgs", "tokens", "age", "tmux-session", "tmux-window",
               "tmux-pane", "hypr-workspace", "pid", "path"]
FILTER_FIELDS = {"path", "tmux", "session", "window", "pane", "time"}

# Long verb forms before their short forms so ordered alternation doesn't
# let `/s` swallow the `s` of `/sort`. The via-form alternative
# (`/fv/field word`) has to come before the generic catch-all line below -
# alternation tries left to right and takes the first that matches at all,
# so if the generic "/fv(?:\s+\S+)?" line came first it would happily
# match just "/fv" (its optional trailing arg requires whitespace, not a
# literal "/") and leave "/field word" to be re-scanned as its own,
# unrelated tokens. See ~/.config/docs/query-dsl.md.
_ARG = r'("[^"]*"|(?!/(?:filter-value|fv|filter-type|ft|add-type|at|remove-type|rt|sort|s|group|g|fts|)(?:/|\s|$))\S+)'
QUERY_ELEM_RE = re.compile(
    r'(!?)(?:'
    r'/(?:filter-value|fv|)/([a-zA-Z,-]+)(?:\s+' + _ARG + r')?'
    r'|/fts(?:/([a-zA-Z,-]+))?(?=\s|$)(?:\s+' + _ARG + r')?'
    r'|/(?:filter-type|ft|add-type|at|remove-type|rt|sort|s|group|g)(?:/\S*)?'  # /g: inert here (fzf cannot fold sections)
    r'|"([^"]*)"'
    r'|(\S+)'
    r')'
)

Query = namedtuple("Query", "terms phrases neg_terms neg_phrases soft field_terms neg_field_terms fts neg_fts fts_scopes sort")


FTS_SCOPE_NAMES = ["claude-live", "claude-archive", "tmux"]
_SCOPE_KEY = {"claude-live": "live", "claude-archive": "archive", "tmux": "tmux"}


def fts_scope_of(keys):
    """`/fts/<keys>` -> set of scopes {"live", "archive", "tmux"}: the keys fuzzy-resolve (exact name wins,
    else substring, comma list unioned) against claude-live / claude-archive / tmux; no keys = all three."""
    if not keys:
        return {"live", "archive", "tmux"}
    hit = dsl.resolve_names(keys, FTS_SCOPE_NAMES)
    return {_SCOPE_KEY[n] for n in hit}


def fts_scope(q):
    """None (no /fts), else the union of every /fts token's scope set."""
    if not q.fts_scopes:
        return None
    return set().union(*q.fts_scopes)


def _sort_val(r, field):
    """A row's value for a /s key, as the strings dsl.compare_field_values expects."""
    if field == "age":
        return f"{max(0, int(time.time() - r['mtime']))}s"   # exact seconds; compare inverts the direction
    v = {"account": _acct_no(r["account"]), "status": STATUS_LABELS.get(r.get("status") or "", r.get("status") or ""),
         "title": r["title"], "msgs": r.get("msgs"), "tokens": r.get("tokens"), "path": r["cwd"],
         "tmux-session": r.get("sess"), "tmux-window": r.get("win"), "tmux-pane": r.get("pane"),
         "hypr-workspace": r.get("wks"), "pid": r.get("pid")}.get(field)
    return "" if v is None else str(v)


def apply_sort(rows, sort):
    """Order rows by a parsed /s (keys, dir); an unresolvable key list leaves them as they are (inert)."""
    keys = dsl.resolve_sort_keys(sort[0], SORT_FIELDS) if sort else None
    if not keys:
        return rows
    import functools
    def cmp(a, b):
        for f in keys:
            c = dsl.compare_field_values(_sort_val(a, f), _sort_val(b, f), sort[1])
            if c:
                return c
        return 0
    return sorted(rows, key=functools.cmp_to_key(cmp))


def resolve_fields(keys):
    """query-dsl.md "Keys" over FIELD_NAMES (shared primitive: ~/.config/dsl/dsl.py)."""
    return dsl.resolve_names(keys, FIELD_NAMES)


def parse_query(query):
    terms, phrases, neg_terms, neg_phrases, soft = [], [], [], [], []
    field_terms, neg_field_terms = [], []
    fts, neg_fts, fts_scopes = [], [], []
    sort = None
    for m in QUERY_ELEM_RE.finditer(query):
        neg, via_field, via_word, fts_keys, fts_arg, quoted, bare = (g or "" for g in m.groups())
        if m.group(0).lstrip("!").startswith("/fts"):
            # `/fts[/keys] [word]`: full text over the conversations. No scope = live AND archived.
            fts_scopes.append(fts_scope_of(fts_keys))
            if fts_arg:
                (neg_fts if neg else fts).append(fts_arg.lower())
            continue
        if m.group(0).lstrip("!")[:1] == "/" and not via_field:
            # /s[/keys[/dir]] orders the rows (the last one wins); /ft /at /rt /g stay inert (fixed columns)
            cmd = dsl.split_cmd(m.group(0).lstrip("!"), {"s": "s", "sort": "s"}, {"s": 3})
            if cmd and not neg and not cmd[3] and cmd[1]:
                sort = (cmd[1].split(","), dsl.parse_direction(cmd[2] or "") or "desc")
            continue
        if via_field:
            fields = resolve_fields(via_field)
            if fields:
                if via_word:
                    (neg_field_terms if neg else field_terms).append((fields, via_word.strip('"').lower()))
                # else: keys alone, no value yet - no-op, deliberately
                # not appended at all (an always-true term would be wrong
                # specifically for the negated form).
                continue
            # unresolvable keys - fall through to plain bare-word handling
            # on the whole "keys word" text (both already consumed by this
            # one regex match) - `tokenize()` splits it back into
            # independent words either way.
            bare = f"{via_field} {via_word}" if via_word else via_field
        if bare:
            toks = tokenize(bare)
            for tok in toks:
                (neg_terms if neg else terms).append(tok)
            if not neg and toks and any(not c.isalnum() for c in bare):
                soft.append(bare.lower())
        elif quoted.strip():
            (neg_phrases if neg else phrases).append(quoted.strip().lower())
    return Query(terms, phrases, neg_terms, neg_phrases, soft, field_terms, neg_field_terms, fts, neg_fts, fts_scopes, sort)


def phrase_hint(raw):
    """The longest whitespace-free literal piece of a phrase - a plain `in`
    test for it is a C-speed pre-filter (7.5MB of corpus text in ~5ms)
    that lets the far slower phrase_re() scan be skipped for every session
    the phrase can't possibly be in. Lowercased like the text it's tested
    against; empty if the phrase is all whitespace (then always "in")."""
    parts = raw.strip().lower().split()
    return max(parts, key=len) if parts else ""


def phrase_re(raw, prefix=False):
    raw = raw.strip().lower()
    parts = [p for p in re.split(r"(\s+)", raw) if p]
    pattern = "".join(r"\s+" if p.isspace() else re.escape(p) for p in parts)
    pre = r"(?<![a-z0-9])" if raw[:1].isalnum() else ""
    post = "" if prefix else (r"(?![a-z0-9])" if raw[-1:].isalnum() else "")
    return re.compile(pre + pattern + post)


CONNECTOR_RE = re.compile(r"^[^\sA-Za-z0-9]+$")


def highlight(text, qset, rxs=()):
    if not qset and not rxs:
        return text
    low = text.lower()
    spans = []
    for rx in rxs:
        for m in rx.finditer(low):
            spans.append((m.start(), m.end()))
    matches = list(TOKEN_RE.finditer(low))
    i = 0
    while i < len(matches):
        if not any(matches[i].group().startswith(q) for q in qset):
            i += 1
            continue
        start, end = matches[i].start(), matches[i].end()
        j = i + 1
        while (j < len(matches)
               and any(matches[j].group().startswith(q) for q in qset)
               and CONNECTOR_RE.match(text[end:matches[j].start()])):
            end = matches[j].end()
            j += 1
        spans.append((start, end))
        i = j
    spans.sort()
    merged = []
    for s, e in spans:
        if merged and s <= merged[-1][1]:
            merged[-1][1] = max(merged[-1][1], e)
        else:
            merged.append([s, e])
    for start, end in reversed(merged):
        text = text[:start] + "\x1b[1;33m" + text[start:end] + "\x1b[0m" + text[end:]
    return text


# --- conversation parsing --------------------------------------------------

def extract_text(content):
    """message.content is either a plain string (typical user turn) or a
    list of content blocks (assistant turns, and user turns carrying a
    tool_result). Only "text" blocks are pulled out - thinking blocks are
    internal monologue nobody searches for by its wording, and tool_use/
    tool_result are the noisiest and largest part of a transcript (whole
    file contents, command output) for the least search value; skipping
    them is what keeps the indexed corpus at a few MB instead of the ~270MB
    these transcripts take up on disk raw."""
    if isinstance(content, str):
        return content
    if isinstance(content, list):
        parts = [b.get("text", "") for b in content
                 if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "text"]
        return "\n".join(p for p in parts if p)
    return ""


def parse_session(path):
    session_id = cwd = ai_title = first_ts = last_ts = None
    turns = []
    msg_count = 0
    try:
        with open(path, errors="replace") as f:
            for line in f:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line:
                    continue
                try:
                    d = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                t = d.get("type")
                if t == "ai-title":
                    ai_title = d.get("aiTitle") or ai_title
                    session_id = session_id or d.get("sessionId")
                    continue
                if t not in ("user", "assistant"):
                    continue
                session_id = session_id or d.get("sessionId") or d.get("session_id")
                cwd = d.get("cwd") or cwd
                ts = d.get("timestamp")
                if ts:
                    first_ts = first_ts or ts
                    last_ts = ts
                msg = d.get("message") or {}
                text = extract_text(msg.get("content")).strip()
                if not text:
                    continue
                msg_count += 1
                if len(text) > MAX_MSG_CHARS:
                    text = text[:MAX_MSG_CHARS] + " …[truncated]"
                turns.append((msg.get("role", t), text))
    except OSError as e:
        _dbg(f"parse_session {path} failed: {e}")
        return None
    if session_id is None or not turns:
        return None
    return {
        "session_id": session_id, "path": path, "cwd": cwd or "?",
        "ai_title": ai_title, "turns": turns, "msg_count": msg_count,
        "first_ts": first_ts, "last_ts": last_ts,
        "mtime": os.path.getmtime(path),
    }


# keyed by CLAUDE_DIR, not just a fixed name: this box runs several
# CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR profiles side by side (~/.claude, ~/.claude2, ...),
# each with its own, disjoint set of project paths under PROJECTS_GLOB. A
# single shared cache file used to mean "switch to a pane on a different
# profile" evicted every entry the other profile had just built - every
# invocation from a differently-profiled pane paid the full
# ProcessPoolExecutor reparse of the whole corpus (~350 files) instead of
# the cheap stat-only path the caching comment above build_snapshot()
# promises. One cache file per profile fixes that: each keeps its own
# entries indefinitely regardless of which other profile ran in between.
_cache_key = "all"  # one corpus for every profile, see project_files()
PARSE_CACHE_PATH = os.path.expanduser(f"~/.cache/claude-history-parse-cache-{_cache_key}.pkl")
# the pre-v5 JSON spelling of the same file - removed on the first save of
# the pickle one so 15MB of dead cache doesn't sit in ~/.cache forever
_LEGACY_CACHE_PATH = PARSE_CACHE_PATH[:-len(".pkl")] + ".json"
# (path, mtime, size) only tells you a *file* hasn't changed - it says
# nothing about whether parse_session() would still
# compute the same thing from it. Bump this whenever their logic changes
# (MAX_MSG_CHARS, extract_text's block filtering,
# etc.) so old entries can't silently outlive the code that produced them;
# a mismatch just means "start over", not corruption.
CACHE_VERSION = 6  # 6: the BM25 tf/doclen fields are gone (full text is Recoll now, see fts_search).
# 2: tf_title/tf_body/time_text added (the $field: DSL).
# 3: the body field was originally named "text" during development, then
# renamed - the cached dict's own keys changed shape (tf_text ->
# tf_body etc.) under what was still version 2, which is exactly the class
# of bug CACHE_VERSION exists to prevent; caught by a KeyError against a
# cache written moments earlier by the pre-rename code, not shipped.
# 4: time_text (the raw ISO timestamp) dropped from what's cached -
# $time: turned out to need the *displayed* relative age ("14d ago"), not
# the raw timestamp, and that has to be computed fresh at query time (it's
# relative to now) rather than ever cached at all - see session_filter_text.
# 5: JSON -> pickle (protocol 5). Same dict, different serializer: loading
# the 15MB JSON was the single largest fixed cost left on the path to the
# first row on screen (0.17s measured) and pickle.load of the same data is
# 0.06s. Nothing else changed shape, but the bump is what makes an old
# .json file get ignored (it's a different path anyway, see
# _LEGACY_CACHE_PATH) rather than needing a format sniff. The file is
# private (mkstemp, 0600) and only ever written by this tool, so
# unpickling it is as trusted as reading its JSON predecessor was.


def load_parse_cache():
    try:
        with open(PARSE_CACHE_PATH, "rb") as f:
            data = pickle.load(f)
        # a version mismatch is treated exactly like "no cache" rather than
        # an error: start over, don't trust entries a different code
        # version produced.
        if not isinstance(data, dict) or data.get("_version") != CACHE_VERSION:
            return {}
        return data.get("files", {})
    except (OSError, ValueError, pickle.UnpicklingError, EOFError, AttributeError):
        return {}


def save_parse_cache(cache):
    # best-effort - the cache is a speed optimization, not a correctness
    # requirement, so a write failure (disk full, permissions) degrades to
    # "next run parses from scratch again", not a crash. Same mkstemp+
    # os.replace pattern as elsewhere in this repo for atomicity - a reader
    # never sees a half-written cache file.
    try:
        d = os.path.dirname(PARSE_CACHE_PATH)
        fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=d, prefix=".claude-history-cache-")
        with os.fdopen(fd, "wb") as f:
            pickle.dump({"_version": CACHE_VERSION, "files": cache}, f, protocol=5)
        os.replace(tmp, PARSE_CACHE_PATH)
        _rm(_LEGACY_CACHE_PATH)
    except OSError:
        pass


def build_snapshot(progress=None):
    """Parsing and indexing every transcript from scratch on every single
    invocation used to be the dominant startup cost - one real file in this
    corpus is 163MB (a runaway conversation with huge tool output; the next
    largest is 16MB). Two independent fixes for the two situations that
    still cost real time:

      - between two invocations a few minutes apart, the overwhelming
        majority of ~170 transcript files haven't changed at all -
        re-parsing all of them every time was pure waste. Cached by
        (path, mtime, size): unchanged files are a stat() and a dict
        lookup, not a re-read; only genuinely new/modified files
        (typically the one or two sessions actually in progress) pay the
        real parse+index cost.
      - the first run ever (or after a corpus-wide change invalidates the
        cache) still has to parse everything, and this is real CPU work
        (JSON-decoding, regex tokenizing) that a thread pool barely
        parallelizes - Python's GIL means only one thread runs Python
        bytecode at a time, so ThreadPoolExecutor mostly just overlaps I/O
        wait, not the CPU-bound part. ProcessPoolExecutor sidesteps the
        GIL entirely (separate interpreters, separate cores) - measured
        1.06s -> 0.65s on this real corpus switching from one to the other.

    `progress`, if given, is called from this thread as each changed file
    finishes parsing - progress(path, indexed_or_None, done_bytes,
    total_bytes) - in completion order, not file order (as_completed, not
    ex.map: one 163MB transcript in this corpus takes longer than every
    other changed file put together, and ex.map would have held every
    result behind it). Byte-weighted rather than file-counted for the same
    reason: 3 of 4 files done can still mean 5% of the work done. This is
    what lets reindex_and_stream() push sessions to an already-open picker
    one at a time instead of only once everything has finished.
    """
    files = project_files()
    if not files:
        die(f"no conversation history found matching {PROJECTS_GLOB}")

    cache = load_parse_cache()
    stats = {}
    to_parse = []
    for path in files:
        try:
            st = os.stat(path)
        except OSError:
            continue
        stats[path] = st
        entry = cache.get(path)
        if not (entry and entry.get("mtime") == st.st_mtime
                and entry.get("size") == st.st_size):
            to_parse.append(path)

    # None is a legitimate result (parse_session found no session id or no
    # turns - an empty/unparseable file) and has to be cached as such, not
    # just omitted: without a cache entry, a permanently-empty file would
    # look identical to "never attempted" on every future run and get
    # re-parsed forever instead of being remembered as not worth indexing.
    freshly_indexed = {}
    if to_parse:
        workers = min(os.cpu_count() or 4, len(to_parse))
        # largest first: with as_completed the order only affects packing,
        # and starting the biggest file first is what keeps the other
        # workers from all sitting idle at the end waiting on it
        to_parse.sort(key=lambda p: -stats[p].st_size)
        total_bytes = sum(stats[p].st_size for p in to_parse) or 1
        done_bytes = 0
        with ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=workers) as ex:
            futs = {ex.submit(parse_session, p): p for p in to_parse}
            for fut in as_completed(futs):
                path = futs[fut]
                s = fut.result()
                indexed = s or None
                freshly_indexed[path] = indexed
                done_bytes += stats[path].st_size
                if progress:
                    progress(path, indexed, done_bytes, total_bytes)

    new_cache = {}
    sessions = {}
    for path in files:
        if path not in stats:
            continue
        indexed = freshly_indexed[path] if path in freshly_indexed else cache[path]["indexed"]
        st = stats[path]
        new_cache[path] = {"mtime": st.st_mtime, "size": st.st_size, "indexed": indexed}
        if indexed:
            sessions[indexed["session_id"]] = indexed
    # writing back ~14MB+ of JSON is pure overhead on the common case (every
    # file was already a cache hit) - only pay for it when the cache itself
    # would actually differ: something got (re)parsed, or a file disappeared
    # from the corpus since the cache was last written.
    if to_parse or len(new_cache) != len(cache):
        save_parse_cache(new_cache)

    return _aggregate_snapshot(sessions)


def _aggregate_snapshot(sessions):
    """The snapshot shape build_snapshot() and build_snapshot_fast() share."""
    return {"sessions": sessions, "n": len(sessions)}


def build_snapshot_fast():
    """The instant-display counterpart to build_snapshot() above: cache
    reads only, zero reparsing, so this is a pickle.load plus a handful of
    stat() calls (~0.1s here) - no ProcessPoolExecutor, no cache rewrite.
    Runs on drive()'s loader thread while fzf is already on screen, so the
    picker never blocks on a cold-cache full corpus parse (measured 2-4s
    on this corpus, worse under memory pressure).

    A file that's missing from the cache entirely (never indexed) is just
    left out of the result - there's nothing to show for it yet. A file
    whose cache entry is stale (changed since last indexed, e.g. a session
    actively being written to right now) still contributes its last-known
    indexed content rather than being dropped, since a slightly-stale row
    beats no row at all for the second or two until the reindex child
    (see reindex_and_stream) streams the fresh version in.

    Returns (snapshot, stale) - stale is True whenever anything in the
    corpus doesn't match the cache byte-for-byte, telling the caller a
    background full build_snapshot() pass is worth kicking off."""
    files = project_files()
    if not files:
        die(f"no conversation history found matching {PROJECTS_GLOB}")

    cache = load_parse_cache()
    stale = False
    sessions = {}
    for path in files:
        try:
            st = os.stat(path)
        except OSError:
            continue
        entry = cache.get(path)
        if not entry:
            stale = True
            continue
        if entry.get("mtime") != st.st_mtime or entry.get("size") != st.st_size:
            stale = True
        indexed = entry["indexed"]
        if indexed:
            sessions[indexed["session_id"]] = indexed
    if len(cache) != len(files):
        stale = True

    return _aggregate_snapshot(sessions), stale


def _send_frame(sock, obj):
    """One length-prefixed pickle on the picker's feed connection (see
    PickerServer's "feed" mode). Best-effort: the picker being gone (user
    already picked something, or dismissed it) is the normal way this
    fails, and is no reason to stop warming the cache."""
    if sock is None:
        return None
    try:
        payload = pickle.dumps(obj, protocol=5)
        sock.sendall(struct.pack("!I", len(payload)) + payload)
        return sock
    except OSError:
        sock.close()
        return None


def reindex_and_stream(server_sock):
    """Run as a detached child (see drive()) after the fast/stale snapshot
    is already on screen: does the real, canonical build_snapshot() -
    reparse whatever changed, rewrite the cache -
    and, while doing it, streams each freshly-parsed session plus a
    byte-weighted progress figure back to the picker over its socket the
    moment it's ready, so the list updates incrementally (and the border
    label counts up) instead of everything landing at once at the end.

    A separate process rather than a thread of the picker on purpose: the
    picker ends in os.execvp (exec_resume), which would take a reindex
    thread and its ProcessPoolExecutor down with it mid-write, and this
    child's other job - warming the cache for the NEXT prefix+C-c - is
    exactly the one that has to survive the user picking something fast.
    Niced so its 12 workers never compete with the picker's own keystroke
    handling for the CPU."""
    try:
        os.nice(10)
    except OSError:
        pass
    sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX)
    try:
        sock.connect(server_sock)
        sock.sendall(b"feed\0")  # mode word, see PickerServer's handler
    except OSError:
        sock.close()
        sock = None  # picker already gone - still worth finishing the cache

    pending = {}
    last_flush = [0.0]

    def progress(path, indexed, done_bytes, total_bytes):
        nonlocal sock
        pending[path] = indexed
        # batch the per-file sends: the picker throttles its own redraws
        # anyway (see PickerState.refresher), so one frame every ~0.3s is
        # as fine-grained as anything downstream can show
        now = time.time()
        if now - last_flush[0] < 0.3 and done_bytes < total_bytes:
            return
        last_flush[0] = now
        sock = _send_frame(sock, {"type": "sessions", "entries": dict(pending),
                                  "done": done_bytes, "total": total_bytes})
        pending.clear()

    snapshot = build_snapshot(progress=progress)
    # the final frame carries the complete session-id set so the picker
    # can also drop sessions whose transcript vanished since its own fast
    # snapshot (a deleted/moved jsonl) - the incremental frames above only
    # ever add or replace
    sock = _send_frame(sock, {"type": "done", "session_ids": list(snapshot["sessions"])})
    if sock:
        sock.close()


# --- live-session detection -------------------------------------------------

def proc_start_ticks(pid):
    """/proc/<pid>/stat field 22 (starttime, clock ticks since boot) - the
    kernel's own answer to "is this really the same process", immune to pid
    reuse. comm (field 2) can contain spaces or parens, so this splits after
    the matching ')' the way procps itself does rather than on whitespace."""
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/stat") as f:
            content = f.read()
        fields = content[content.rindex(")") + 2:].split()
        return fields[19]  # field 22, minus the 3 consumed (pid, comm, state)
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return None


def get_live_sessions():
    """session_id -> {pid, pane_id, status, cwd} for every session whose
    sessions/<pid>.json still names a pid that (a) exists and (b) has the
    same /proc start-time procStart records - see module docstring for why
    both checks matter. pane_id is parsed out of the "session:@win.%pane"
    tmux field tmux(1) accepts a bare %pane_id as a fully-qualified target,
    so nothing else about the window/session needs resolving."""
    live = {}
    for jf in glob.glob(SESSIONS_GLOB):
        try:
            with open(jf) as f:
                d = json.load(f)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
        pid, proc_start, sid = d.get("pid"), d.get("procStart"), d.get("sessionId")
        if not (pid and proc_start and sid):
            continue
        if proc_start_ticks(pid) != str(proc_start):
            continue  # dead, or the pid was recycled by something else
        tmux_field = d.get("tmux") or ""
        pane_id = None
        if "." in tmux_field:
            tail = tmux_field.rsplit(".", 1)[-1]
            if tail.startswith("%"):
                pane_id = tail
        live[sid] = {"pid": pid, "pane_id": pane_id,
                     "status": d.get("status", "?"), "cwd": d.get("cwd")}
    return live


# --- rendering ---------------------------------------------------------------

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")


def shorten(path):
    if not path:
        return "?"
    return "~" + path[len(HOME):] if path.startswith(HOME) else path


def relative_age(mtime):
    secs = time.time() - mtime
    if secs < 60:
        return "just now"
    if secs < 3600:
        return f"{int(secs // 60)}m ago"
    if secs < 86400:
        return f"{int(secs // 3600)}h ago"
    return f"{int(secs // 86400)}d ago"


STATUS_COLOR = {"busy": "\x1b[1;33m", "waiting": "\x1b[1;33m"}  # default: green

# --- search / preview / complete (served over the picker's socket on every
# keystroke - see PickerServer; same shape as window-search.py's
# --search/--preview, minus the per-keystroke process) --------------------

def session_filter_text(sid, s, live_map, targets):
    """FILTER_FIELDS text (lowercased) for one session, computed once per
    session per search() call rather than per field-term - $session:/
    $window:/$pane: are the individual pieces $tmux: is built from (see
    pane_targets()), not re-split out of the combined string.

    "time" is relative_age(s["mtime"]) - the exact string the TIME column
    displays ("14d ago", "45m ago") - not the raw timestamp: a first
    version searched the raw ISO string instead, which technically worked
    but not for what anyone actually typing $time:14 while looking at a
    "14d ago" row expects it to mean. Computed fresh here rather than
    cached in the snapshot, same reason row() computes it fresh too - it's
    relative to *now*, not a fixed fact about the session."""
    live = live_map.get(sid)
    entry = None
    if live and live.get("pane_id"):
        entry = targets.get(live["pane_id"])
    return {
        "path": (s["cwd"] or "").lower(),
        "time": relative_age(s["mtime"]).lower(),
        "tmux": entry["loc"].lower() if entry else "",
        "session": entry["session"].lower() if entry else "",
        "window": entry["window"].lower() if entry else "",
        "pane": entry["pane"].lower() if entry else "",
    }


# Columns shared with the ctrl+alt+c usage panel (one layout for both):
#   live view    ACCT STATUS TITLE MSGS TKNS AGE SESS WIN PANE WKS PID PATH
#   /fts/archive ACCT TITLE MSGS TKNS AGE PATH   (what only a running session has is dropped)
STATUS_LABELS = {"busy": "busy", "waiting": "wait", "idle": "idle"}
W_ACCT, W_STATUS, W_TITLE, W_TKNS, W_MSGS, W_AGE, W_TMUX, W_WKS, W_PID = 4, 6, 38, 5, 5, 8, 4, 4, 7


def _clip(text, width):
    text = " ".join((text or "").replace("✳", " ").split())
    return text if len(text) <= width else text[:width - 1] + "…"


def _age(mtime):
    return relative_age(mtime).replace(" ago", "")


def _acct_no(account):
    """The panel's bare account number: claude -> 1, claude6 -> 6."""
    if not account:
        return ""
    digits = "".join(ch for ch in account if ch.isdigit())
    return digits or "1"


def _tkns(n):
    return "" if not n else (f"{n / 1000:.0f}k" if n >= 1000 else str(n))


def live_rows():
    """Running sessions, newest first, from the usage daemon's state (the
    panel's own source: account, status, tokens, tmux ids, workspace, pid);
    falls back to the sessions registry if the daemon isn't running."""
    rows = []
    try:
        state = json.load(open(os.path.expanduser("~/.cache/claude-usage/state.json")))
        for acct, lst in (state.get("sessions") or {}).items():
            for r in lst:
                rows.append({"sid": r.get("session_id") or "", "account": acct, "status": r.get("status") or "",
                             "title": r.get("title") or "", "tokens": r.get("context_tokens"),
                             "mtime": (r.get("updated_at_ms") or 0) / 1000, "sess": r.get("tmux_session") or "",
                             "win": r.get("tmux_window") or "", "pane": r.get("tmux_pane") or "",
                             "wks": r.get("hypr_workspace") or "", "pid": r.get("pid") or "",
                             "cwd": r.get("cwd") or ""})
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        for sid, l in get_live_sessions().items():
            rows.append({"sid": sid, "account": account_name(find_session_profile(sid)), "status": l.get("status") or "",
                         "title": "", "tokens": None, "mtime": time.time(), "sess": "", "win": "", "pane": l.get("pane_id") or "",
                         "wks": "", "pid": l.get("pid") or "", "cwd": l.get("cwd") or ""})
    try:    # message counts come from the indexer's meta.json (a couple of minutes behind a live session)
        meta = json.load(open(os.path.expanduser("~/.cache/indexes/claude/meta.json")))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        meta = {}
    for r in rows:
        r["msgs"] = (meta.get(r["sid"]) or {}).get("msgs") or ""
    rows = [r for r in rows if r["sid"]]
    rows.sort(key=lambda r: -r["mtime"])
    return rows


def _row_text(r):
    return " ".join([r["account"], r["title"], r["cwd"], relative_age(r["mtime"]), r.get("sess", ""),
                     r.get("win", ""), r.get("pane", "")]).lower()


def _fmt_live(r):
    return "  ".join([
        f'{_acct_no(r["account"]):<{W_ACCT}}', f'{STATUS_LABELS.get(r.get("status", ""), r.get("status", "")):<{W_STATUS}}',
        f'{_clip(r["title"], W_TITLE):<{W_TITLE}}', f'{r["msgs"]!s:>{W_MSGS}}', f'{_tkns(r["tokens"]):>{W_TKNS}}',
        f'{_age(r["mtime"]):>{W_AGE}}', f'{r.get("sess", ""):<{W_TMUX}}', f'{r.get("win", ""):<{W_TMUX}}',
        f'{r.get("pane", ""):<{W_TMUX}}', f'{r.get("wks", ""):<{W_WKS}}', f'{r.get("pid", "")!s:<{W_PID}}', shorten(r["cwd"])])


def _fmt_archive(r):
    return "  ".join([
        f'{_acct_no(r["account"]):<{W_ACCT}}', f'{_clip(r["title"], W_TITLE):<{W_TITLE}}',
        f'{r["msgs"]:>{W_MSGS}}', f'{_tkns(r["tokens"]):>{W_TKNS}}',
        f'{_age(r["mtime"]):>{W_AGE}}', shorten(r["cwd"])])


HEADER_LIVE = (f'{"ACCT":<{W_ACCT}}  {"STATUS":<{W_STATUS}}  {"TITLE":<{W_TITLE}}  {"MSGS":>{W_MSGS}}  {"TKNS":>{W_TKNS}}  {"AGE":>{W_AGE}}  '
               f'{"SESS":<{W_TMUX}}  {"WIN":<{W_TMUX}}  {"PANE":<{W_TMUX}}  {"WKS":<{W_WKS}}  {"PID":<{W_PID}}  PATH')
HEADER_ARCHIVE = (f'{"ACCT":<{W_ACCT}}  {"TITLE":<{W_TITLE}}  {"MSGS":>{W_MSGS}}  {"TKNS":>{W_TKNS}}  {"AGE":>{W_AGE}}  PATH')


def search(snap, query):
    """The fzf result lines ("session_id<TAB>label") for `query`. Default
    view: the running sessions, in the usage panel's columns. `/fts word`
    searches the conversations' text, live AND archived (best match first);
    `/fts/live word` only the running ones, `/fts/archive [word]` only past
    ones (own, shorter columns). Bare words, "phrases" and !neg are substring
    filters over the row's own values. Called on every keystroke by the
    picker's socket server."""
    q = parse_query(query)
    scope = fts_scope(q)

    words = list(q.terms)
    for x in q.soft:           # a punctuated word was split by parse_query; use it whole
        for tok in tokenize(x):
            if tok in words:
                words.remove(tok)
    table_pos = words + list(q.phrases) + list(q.soft)
    table_neg = list(q.neg_terms) + list(q.neg_phrases)
    body_terms = [t for f, t in q.field_terms if f == ["body"]]
    neg_body_terms = [t for f, t in q.neg_field_terms if f == ["body"]]
    pos = list(q.fts) + body_terms
    neg = list(q.neg_fts) + neg_body_terms
    ftsq = " ".join(pos + ["!" + n for n in neg])

    live_on = scope is None or "live" in scope
    arch_on = scope is not None and "archive" in scope
    tmux_on = scope is not None and "tmux" in scope
    archive_only = scope == {"archive"}
    fmt = _fmt_archive if archive_only else _fmt_live
    base = []
    if live_on:
        base = live_rows()
        if pos or neg:
            hits = dict(fts_search(ftsq)) if pos else None
            if hits is None:
                excluded = {sid for sid, _ in fts_search(" ".join(neg))}
                base = [r for r in base if r["sid"] not in excluded]
            else:
                base = [dict(r, score=hits[r["sid"]]) for r in base if r["sid"] in hits]
    extra = []
    if arch_on and (pos or neg or archive_only):
        extra += session_rows(ftsq, "archive")
    if tmux_on and pos:
        extra += session_rows(ftsq, "tmux")
    if extra:
        for r in extra:
            r["sid"] = r["session_id"]
        base = extra if archive_only else sorted(base + extra, key=lambda r: -r.get("score", 0))

    base = apply_sort(base, q.sort)
    other = [(f, t, False) for f, t in q.field_terms if f != ["body"]] + \
            [(f, t, True) for f, t in q.neg_field_terms if f != ["body"]]
    out = []
    for r in base:
        text = _row_text(r)
        if not all(t in text for t in table_pos) or any(t in text for t in table_neg):
            continue
        status = r.get("status") or ""
        vals = {"path": r["cwd"], "title": r["title"], "time": relative_age(r["mtime"]),
                "status": STATUS_LABELS.get(status, status) if status else "archive",
                "acct": _acct_no(r["account"]),
                "tmux": f'{r.get("sess", "")}:{r.get("win", "")}.{r.get("pane", "")}',
                "session": r.get("sess", ""), "window": r.get("win", ""), "pane": r.get("pane", "")}
        # //keys value, !//keys value: the term must (not) appear in one of the resolved types
        if not all(any(term in str(vals.get(f, "")).lower() for f in fields if f in vals) != neg
                   for fields, term, neg in other):
            continue
        out.append(f'{r["sid"]}\t{fmt(r)}')
    return out


# --- Tab completion (bound to `tab` in drive(), see query-dsl.md's "Via
# paths") -------------------------------------------------------------

def _completion_context(query):
    """(frag, prev, trailing_space): `frag` is the token currently being
    typed (empty right after a trailing space, at a fresh argument
    position); `prev` is the whitespace-delimited token immediately before
    it (`None` at the very start of the query). No quote-awareness here,
    unlike the real DSL tokenizer - completion only ever needs to look at
    `/fv/...` shaped tokens, which are never quoted."""
    trailing_space = query == "" or query[-1].isspace()
    toks = query.split()
    if trailing_space:
        return "", (toks[-1] if toks else None), True
    frag = toks[-1] if toks else ""
    prev = toks[-2] if len(toks) > 1 else None
    return frag, prev, False


def _value_candidates(fields, fragment, snap, live_map, targets):
    """Every distinct, non-empty FILTER_FIELDS value across all sessions
    for the resolved `fields`, substring-narrowed by `fragment` - the same
    "live values, deduped" corpus the GTK pickers' own Value stage draws
    from (see query.rs::value_suggestions)."""
    fragment = fragment.lower()
    seen = set()
    for sid, s in snap["sessions"].items():
        filter_text = session_filter_text(sid, s, live_map, targets)
        for f in fields:
            v = filter_text.get(f, "")
            if v and fragment in v:
                seen.add(v)
    return sorted(seen)


def complete_query(query, snap):
    """The one Tab completion available at the end of `query`, as
    {"query", "prefix", "suffix", "candidates"}: the completed query is
    prefix + <chosen candidate> + suffix, or `query` unchanged if there's
    nothing to complete (candidates empty - a `transform-query` no-op back
    in fzf). The *choosing* happens in the client (CLIENT_SRC), not here:
    a unique candidate completes directly, no popup - same "narrow match
    completes silently, ambiguous one shows a list" rule ordinary shell
    tab-completion follows - and 2+ candidates open a small nested fzf
    there (fzf's `execute(...)` action hands over the terminal to whatever
    it runs, same as `execute(less {})` in fzf's own docs, so a real
    interactive picker works even though it's launched *from inside* the
    outer fzf; the client is what's running on that terminal, this server
    thread isn't). Stages (query-dsl.md "Autocompletion"): verb (`/` ->
    fts, fts/claude-live, fts/claude-archive and every fv/field), key
    (`/fv/<frag>` / `//<frag>`, after the last comma) and `/fts/<scope>`,
    and the value after `/fv/keys `."""
    frag, prev, _ = _completion_context(query)
    none = {"query": query, "prefix": "", "suffix": "", "candidates": []}

    if frag.startswith("/"):
        rest = frag[1:]
        if "/" in rest:
            name, keys = rest.split("/", 1)
            if "/" in keys:
                return none
            comma = keys.rfind(",")
            key_frag = keys[comma + 1:]
            prefix = query[: len(query) - len(key_frag)]
            if name == "fts":   # /fts/<scope names>
                scopes = [x for x in ("claude", "claude-live", "claude-archive", "tmux") if key_frag.lower() in x]
                if scopes:
                    return {"query": query, "prefix": prefix, "suffix": " ", "candidates": scopes}
                return none
            if name in ("fv", "filter-value", ""):
                # a complete name wins over its fuzzy siblings in the *typed* key, but for
                # completion every name containing the fragment is offered
                candidates = [f for f in FIELD_NAMES if f != "body" and key_frag.lower() in f]
                if candidates:
                    return {"query": query, "prefix": prefix, "suffix": " ", "candidates": candidates}
            return none
        # `/frag` - completing the verb itself, crossed with every field
        # it can scope to ("Verb-stage depth", query-dsl.md): one Tab from
        # bare "/" already reaches the full flat "/fv/field" list.
        rest_lower = rest.lower()
        candidates = [v for v in ("fts", "fts/claude", "fts/claude-live", "fts/claude-archive", "fts/tmux") if rest_lower in v]
        candidates += [f"fv/{f}" for f in FIELD_NAMES if f != "body" and rest_lower in f"fv/{f}"]
        if candidates:
            return {"query": query, "prefix": query[: len(query) - len(frag)] + "/",
                    "suffix": " ", "candidates": candidates}
        return none

    if prev and prev.startswith("/"):
        parts = prev[1:].split("/")
        if len(parts) == 2 and parts[0] in ("fv", "filter-value", "") and parts[1]:
            fields = [f for f in resolve_fields(parts[1]) if f in FILTER_FIELDS]
            if fields and snap:
                live_map = get_live_sessions()
                targets = pane_targets() if in_tmux() else {}
                candidates = _value_candidates(fields, frag, snap, live_map, targets)
                if candidates:
                    prefix = query[: len(query) - len(frag)] if frag else query
                    return {"query": query, "prefix": prefix, "suffix": " ", "candidates": candidates}
    return none


PREVIEW_CONTEXT_TURNS = 2  # turns of leading context kept above a jumped-to match


def preview(session_id, query, snap):
    if session_id.startswith("tmux:"):
        q = parse_query(query)
        words = " ".join(w.strip('"') for w in q.fts)
        lines = [f"tmux pane {session_id[5:]} - scrollback matches", "-" * 60]
        for ex in fts_snippets(session_id, words, limit=12) if words else []:
            lines.append(ex.replace("\x01", "\x1b[1;33m").replace("\x02", "\x1b[0m"))
            lines.append("")
        return "\n".join(lines)
    if not snap:
        return "[snapshot unavailable]"
    s = snap["sessions"].get(session_id)
    if not s:
        return "[session not found in snapshot]"
    out = []

    q = parse_query(query)
    fts_text = " ".join(q.fts).replace('"', " ")
    qset = set(tokenize(fts_text)) | {tok for _, term in q.field_terms for tok in tokenize(term)}
    rxs = [phrase_re(p, prefix=True) for p in q.fts if p.startswith('"')]

    live_map = get_live_sessions()
    live = live_map.get(session_id)
    header = f"{shorten(s['cwd'])}  ·  {s['msg_count']} messages  ·  {relative_age(s['mtime'])}"
    if live:
        loc = None
        if live["pane_id"] and in_tmux():
            entry = pane_targets().get(live["pane_id"])
            loc = entry["loc"] if entry else None
        where = loc or (f"pane {live['pane_id']} (different tmux server)"
                        if live["pane_id"] else "not in tmux")
        header += f"\n\x1b[1;32m● running now\x1b[0m (pid {live['pid']}, {live['status']}, {where})"
    out.append(header)
    out.append("─" * 60)

    turns = s["turns"]
    match_idx = None
    if qset or rxs:
        for i in range(len(turns) - 1, -1, -1):
            low = turns[i][1].lower()
            if any(rx.search(low) for rx in rxs):
                match_idx = i
                break
            if qset and any(t.startswith(q_) for t in tokenize(low) for q_ in qset):
                match_idx = i
                break

    start = 0
    if match_idx is not None and match_idx > PREVIEW_CONTEXT_TURNS:
        start = match_idx - PREVIEW_CONTEXT_TURNS
        out.append(f"… {start} earlier message(s) not shown …\n")

    for role, text in turns[start:]:
        who = "You" if role == "user" else "Claude"
        out.append(f"\x1b[1m{who}:\x1b[0m {highlight(text, qset, rxs)}\n")
    return "\n".join(out)


# --- jump / resume -----------------------------------------------------------

def die(msg):
    _dbg(f"FATAL: {msg}")
    print(f"claude-history: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(1)


def in_tmux():
    # $TMUX_PANE is set by tmux for every process started inside a pane
    # (no bind-key plumbing needed to learn "which pane am I", unlike the
    # tmux-popup version of this tool which had to have #{pane_id}
    # interpolated in by the key binding) - both are unset outside tmux, so
    # this is also the "is tmux even available" check the module docstring
    # describes: no separate probe for a running tmux server needed.
    return bool(os.environ.get("TMUX")) and bool(os.environ.get("TMUX_PANE"))


def current_client_tty():
    """The tty of the client viewing THIS pane, resolved from directly
    inside the pane's own process tree - not via a bind-key's run-shell (see
    window-search.py's saga on why that ambient form is unreliable there).
    Run as a plain child of the pane's shell, tmux resolves "#{client_tty}"
    the same as if you'd typed the command at the prompt yourself, so this
    is reliable without any bridging."""
    r = subprocess.run(["tmux", "display-message", "-p", "#{client_tty}"],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else None


def pane_targets():
    """pane_id -> {"session", "window", "pane", "loc"} for every pane on the
    CURRENT tmux server - one list-panes call, reused for "does this live
    session's pane exist on this server" (membership test), the row()
    location column ("loc", tmux's own session:window.pane convention), and
    the $session:/$window:/$pane:/$tmux: DSL fields (the individual pieces
    "loc" is built from - see search()), so nothing pays for a second
    subprocess call or has to re-split "loc" back apart."""
    r = subprocess.run(["tmux", "list-panes", "-a", "-F",
                        "#{pane_id}\t#{session_name}\t#{window_index}\t#{pane_index}"],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return {}
    targets = {}
    for line in r.stdout.splitlines():
        parts = line.split("\t")
        if len(parts) != 4:
            continue
        pane_id, session, window, pane = parts
        targets[pane_id] = {"session": session, "window": window, "pane": pane,
                            "loc": f"{session}:{window}.{pane}"}
    return targets


def jump_to_pane(client, pane_id):
    """Land the client on an already-running session's pane. Same verified
    sequence as this repo's window-search.py jump() (switch-client -c, then
    select-window/select-pane, then re-checked against a fresh list-clients
    rather than trusting the return code) - hard-won there, reused here."""
    cr = subprocess.run(["tmux", "list-clients", "-F", "#{client_tty}\t#{session_name}"],
                        capture_output=True, text=True)
    if cr.returncode != 0:
        die(f"tmux list-clients failed (rc={cr.returncode}): {cr.stderr.strip()}")
    clients = {c: sn for c, sn in (line.split("\t") for line in cr.stdout.splitlines())}
    if client not in clients:
        die(f"client {client!r} isn't in the current attached-client list "
            f"{sorted(clients)!r} - can't target a switch-client to it")

    for cmd in (["switch-client", "-c", client, "-t", pane_id],
                ["select-window", "-t", pane_id],
                ["select-pane", "-t", pane_id]):
        r = subprocess.run(["tmux", *cmd], capture_output=True, text=True)
        if r.returncode != 0:
            die(f"tmux {' '.join(cmd)} failed: {r.stderr.strip()}")

    after = {c: p for c, p in (
        line.split("\t") for line in
        subprocess.run(["tmux", "list-clients", "-F", "#{client_tty}\t#{pane_id}"],
                        capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.splitlines()
    )}
    if after.get(client) != pane_id:
        die(f"selected {pane_id} but client {client} ended up on "
            f"{after.get(client)!r} instead - switch-client silently landed "
            f"on the wrong pane")


def find_session_profile(session_id):
    """The CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR (account) this session was last used under, or
    None. projects/ and history.jsonl are symlinked between ~/.claude{,2,3}
    so they say nothing about the account; session-env/<id> and
    file-history/<id> are per-profile real dirs, newest mtime wins (a
    session can be resumed under a different account). A live session's
    registry (sessions/<pid>.json) is authoritative if present."""
    for jf in glob.glob(SESSIONS_GLOB):
        try:
            with open(jf) as f:
                if json.load(f).get("sessionId") == session_id:
                    return os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(jf))
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
    best, best_m = None, -1
    # Most reliable for a just-closed session: each profile's .claude.json
    # records projects[cwd].lastSessionId, and session-env/file-history
    # dirs often don't exist (no tool use / no file edits). Newest
    # .claude.json wins if several profiles name the session.
    for d in glob.glob(os.path.expanduser("~/.claude*")):
        cj = os.path.join(d, ".claude.json")
        try:
            with open(cj) as f:
                projs = json.load(f).get("projects") or {}
            if any(isinstance(v, dict) and v.get("lastSessionId") == session_id
                   for v in projs.values()):
                m = os.stat(cj).st_mtime
                if m > best_m:
                    best, best_m = d, m
        except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
            continue
    if best:
        return best
    for d in glob.glob(os.path.expanduser("~/.claude*")):
        for sub in ("session-env", "file-history"):
            try:
                m = os.stat(os.path.join(d, sub, session_id)).st_mtime
            except OSError:
                continue
            if m > best_m:
                best, best_m = d, m
    return best


ACCOUNT_NAMES = {".claude2": "claude2", ".claude3": "claude3", ".claude4": "claude4", ".claude5": "claude5",
                 ".claude6": "claude6", ".claude7": "claude7", ".claude8": "claude8"}


def account_name(profile):
    return ACCOUNT_NAMES.get(os.path.basename((profile or CLAUDE_DIR).rstrip("/")), "claude")


def profile_map():
    """session_id -> profile dir (account) it was last used under, for every
    session at once (find_session_profile's rules, one pass instead of one
    per session): a profile's .claude.json projects[cwd].lastSessionId
    (newest .claude.json wins), else its session-env / file-history dirs
    (newest mtime wins)."""
    best = {}
    for d in glob.glob(os.path.expanduser("~/.claude*")):
        cj = os.path.join(d, ".claude.json")
        try:
            with open(cj) as f:
                projs = json.load(f).get("projects") or {}
            m = os.stat(cj).st_mtime
        except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
            continue
        for v in projs.values():
            sid = isinstance(v, dict) and v.get("lastSessionId")
            if sid and (sid not in best or m > best[sid][1]):
                best[sid] = (d, m + 1e12)       # a .claude.json record outranks any dir mtime
    for d in glob.glob(os.path.expanduser("~/.claude*")):
        for sub in ("session-env", "file-history"):
            try:
                with os.scandir(os.path.join(d, sub)) as it:
                    for e in it:
                        m = e.stat().st_mtime
                        if e.name not in best or m > best[e.name][1]:
                            best[e.name] = (d, m)
            except OSError:
                continue
    return {sid: d for sid, (d, _) in best.items()}


def label_window(profile, pane=None):
    """Name this pane's window after the account (claude/claude2/claude3/claude4)
    the way claude-account-window-rename-hook.sh does, and mark it as
    managed (@claude_autoname/@claude_lastset) so that hook/watcher keep
    it current afterwards. The exec below makes claude this pane's process
    without any window-renamed event we could rely on, so it's done here."""
    pane = pane or os.environ.get("TMUX_PANE")
    if not pane:
        return
    name = account_name(profile)
    subprocess.run(["tmux", "rename-window", "-t", pane, name],
                   stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    subprocess.run(["tmux", "set-window-option", "-t", pane,
                    "@claude_autoname", "1"], stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    subprocess.run(["tmux", "set-window-option", "-t", pane,
                    "@claude_lastset", name], stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)


def exec_resume(session_id, cwd):
    """Replace this process with `claude --resume ...` - this program was
    launched directly as a shell's foreground job (a plain CLI, not
    something spawned via tmux run-shell/popup), so exec is the literal
    "resume in the current shell" the whole tool exists to do: no pane
    targeting, no send-keys, no new window - just hand the terminal
    straight to claude the way `exec` hands it to anything else."""
    os.chdir(cwd if cwd and os.path.isdir(cwd) else HOME)
    profile = find_session_profile(session_id)
    if profile:
        os.environ["CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"] = profile
    label_window(profile)
    os.execvp("claude", ["claude", "--resume", session_id,
                         "--dangerously-skip-permissions"])


def close_origin_pane(target_pane_id):
    """Close the PANE claude-history was invoked from (not its window - a
    prior version killed the whole window here, which took out whatever
    else was split into it too), now that the client has been switched
    away from it. Only called right after a successful jump_to_pane in the
    plain-CLI path (resolve()), which assumes this pane was just a scratch
    launcher - see resolve()'s docstring. Skipped if the target somehow
    turns out to be this same pane (should be impossible - the live
    session detected is always a different process - but kill-pane on the
    pane you were just switched to would be exactly backwards, so this
    stays defensive rather than assumed)."""
    origin_pane = os.environ.get("TMUX_PANE")
    if not origin_pane or origin_pane == target_pane_id:
        return
    subprocess.run(["tmux", "kill-pane", "-t", origin_pane])


def resolve(session_id, cwd):
    """This program is always running as a shell's own foreground job -
    typed directly at a prompt, or (prefix+C-c, .tmux.conf) started via
    `new-window`, which is exactly the same thing from this process's own
    point of view - so a pane, if any, was opened specifically to run this
    search and is fair game to close once its job is done."""
    if not in_tmux():
        # no tmux to integrate with - skip the live/switch machinery
        # entirely and just resume here, per the tool's own fallback rule
        exec_resume(session_id, cwd)
        return

    live = get_live_sessions().get(session_id)  # re-checked fresh: time
    # passed while the user was browsing/searching, status may have changed
    if live and live["pane_id"] and live["pane_id"] in pane_targets():
        client = current_client_tty()
        if not client:
            die("tmux display-message couldn't resolve #{client_tty} for "
                "this pane - can't switch-client without it")
        jump_to_pane(client, live["pane_id"])
        print(f"claude-history: switched to the already-running session "
              f"(pid {live['pid']}, {live['status']})", file=sys.stderr)
        # only on the switch path - never when falling back to exec_resume,
        # per the tool's own rule (see module docstring)
        close_origin_pane(live["pane_id"])
        return
    if live:
        where = (f"pane {live['pane_id']} (a different tmux server)"
                 if live["pane_id"] else "not attached to any tmux pane")
        print(f"claude-history: session is already running elsewhere "
              f"(pid {live['pid']}, cwd {live['cwd']}, {where}) - not "
              f"resuming, that would start a second instance against the "
              f"same transcript", file=sys.stderr)
        return
    resume_tmux(session_id)  # past conversation: its own new tmux window, under its own account


SNAP_PREFIX = "claude-history-search-"
# Search-box history (query-dsl.md's "Search-box history" section): one
# submitted query per line, oldest first - fzf's own --history file
# format, loaded straight into drive()'s outer fzf below so ctrl-p/ctrl-n
# ("Up-arrow"'s equivalent here - Up/Down stay list-navigation, same
# reasoning as focus-picker.py's HISTORY_PATH comment) cycle through it
# and draft-restore for free, confirmed directly against real fzf
# 2026-09-13. Ctrl+R (history_candidates/CLIENT_SRC below) is a second,
# separate nested-fzf popup over the same file, fuzzy-matched.
HISTORY_PATH = os.path.expanduser("~/.cache/claude-history-query-history")


def dedupe_history():
    """Same HIST_IGNORE_ALL_DUPS + HIST_REDUCE_BLANKS collapse as
    focus-picker.py's dedupe_history() - fzf's --history just appends on
    every accept with no dedup at all (confirmed directly), so this keeps
    only the LAST occurrence of each whitespace-normalized line, run after
    the outer fzf exits so both the next invocation's --history load and
    this invocation's own already-fresh Ctrl+R reads see a clean file."""
    try:
        with open(HISTORY_PATH) as f:
            lines = f.read().splitlines()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return
    last_pos, order = {}, []
    for idx, line in enumerate(lines):
        norm = " ".join(line.split())
        if norm:
            last_pos[norm] = idx
    for norm in last_pos:
        order.append(norm)
    order.sort(key=lambda norm: last_pos[norm])
    d = os.path.dirname(HISTORY_PATH)
    fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=d, prefix=".claude-history-history-")
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as f:
            f.write("\n".join(order) + ("\n" if order else ""))
        os.chmod(tmp, 0o600)
        os.replace(tmp, HISTORY_PATH)
    except OSError:
        _rm(tmp)


def history_candidates(query):
    """{"query", "candidates"} for Ctrl+R: HISTORY_PATH read fresh (this
    server process is long-lived for the picker's whole session, unlike
    focus-picker.py's one-shot invocation - see query-dsl.md's
    INC_APPEND_HISTORY/SHARE_HISTORY note), most-recent-first. The client
    (CLIENT_SRC) does the actual nested-fzf popup and whole-line replace,
    same "server decides the data, client owns the terminal" split
    complete_query already uses."""
    try:
        with open(HISTORY_PATH) as f:
            entries = [l for l in f.read().splitlines() if l.strip()]
    except FileNotFoundError:
        entries = []
    entries.reverse()
    return {"query": query, "candidates": entries}


def snapshot_dir():
    xdg = os.environ.get("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR")
    if xdg and os.path.isdir(xdg) and os.access(xdg, os.W_OK):
        return xdg
    return tempfile.gettempdir()


def _rm(path):
    try:
        os.unlink(path)
    except OSError:
        pass


def sweep_stale_snapshots():
    """Same reasoning as window-search.py's sweep - the picker's per-pid
    files (its own request socket, fzf's --listen socket, the Tab handoff
    file) all sit in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR under one pid-prefixed name each, and
    an interrupted invocation (second C-c SIGHUPing the first, a crash)
    leaks them; anything named for a pid that no longer exists is fair
    game. (These used to include a 16MB snapshot json per invocation - the
    snapshot lives in memory now - and the .txt handoff file used to be
    missed here entirely, so a crashed picker left it behind for good.)"""
    seen = set()
    for d in (snapshot_dir(), tempfile.gettempdir()):
        if d in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(d)
        try:
            names = os.listdir(d)
        except OSError:
            continue
        for name in names:
            if not (name.startswith(SNAP_PREFIX)
                    and name.endswith((".json", ".sock", ".txt", ".py"))):
                continue
            try:
                pid = int(name[len(SNAP_PREFIX):].split("-", 1)[0])
            except ValueError:
                continue
            try:
                os.kill(pid, 0)
                continue
            except ProcessLookupError:
                pass
            except OSError:
                continue
            _rm(os.path.join(d, name))


# --- the picker's own socket server -----------------------------------------
#
# fzf can only get its rows from a command it runs, and it runs that
# command afresh on every keystroke. Before this, that command was this
# very script (`--search <snapshot.json>`), which meant every keystroke
# paid a fresh interpreter start plus json.load of a 16MB snapshot - 0.34s
# before one row printed, then the same again for the preview. Now the
# snapshot lives in drive()'s process for the life of the picker and fzf's
# per-keystroke command is CLIENT_SRC below, written out to a per-pid
# file next to the sockets and run as `python3 -I -S <file>` (~10ms: -I -S
# skip site/env setup, and the file is tiny; as a `-c` string it would
# have had to survive being nested inside fzf's own `reload(...)`
# delimiters, which the parentheses in it wouldn't) - it forwards its argv
# over a unix socket and copies the reply to stdout. Same three operations
# (search / preview / complete), same output, just without re-reading
# anything from disk per keystroke.
#
# The same socket has a fourth mode, "feed", that the detached reindex
# child (reindex_and_stream) streams freshly-parsed sessions and progress
# into - see PickerState for how those are folded into the live view.

CLIENT_SRC = r"""
import socket,sys
s=socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX);s.connect(sys.argv[1])
s.sendall("\0".join(sys.argv[2:]).encode());s.shutdown(socket.SHUT_WR)
b=bytearray()
while (c:=s.recv(1<<16)):b+=c
mode=sys.argv[2]
if mode not in ("complete","history"):sys.stdout.buffer.write(b);sys.exit()
import json,subprocess
r=json.loads(b);q=r["query"];c=r["candidates"]
if mode=="complete":
    if len(c)==1:q=r["prefix"]+c[0]+r["suffix"]
    elif c:
        p=subprocess.run(["fzf","--height=100%","--layout=reverse","--border=rounded","--prompt=complete> ","--bind","ctrl-space:put( )","--bind","tab:accept"],input="\n".join(c),capture_output=True,text=True)
        if p.stdout.strip():q=r["prefix"]+p.stdout.strip()+r["suffix"]
else:
    # history: always opens, even with zero candidates - zsh's own ctrl-r
    # widget shows its popup unconditionally too, rather than doing
    # nothing the first time it's pressed with no history yet (an
    # earlier version skipped opening here, which looked indistinguishable
    # from Ctrl+R being unbound - reported 2026-09-13). Seeded with the
    # current query (fzf --query "$LBUFFER"'s own behaviour) and a
    # whole-line replace on accept, no prefix/suffix.
    p=subprocess.run(["fzf","--height=100%","--layout=reverse","--border=rounded","--prompt=history> ","--query="+q,"--bind","tab:accept"],input="\n".join(c),capture_output=True,text=True)
    if p.stdout.strip():q=p.stdout.strip()
sys.stdout.write(q)
""".strip()


def fzf_post(sock_path, action):
    """POST one action string to fzf's --listen socket. http.client over
    AF_UNIX rather than a curl subprocess: this runs from the refresher
    thread up to a few times a second while the reindex streams in, and
    a 10ms fork+exec per poke is the kind of cost this whole rewrite is
    about removing. Returns whether fzf took it: False both when fzf is
    gone (normal at the end) and when it hasn't bound the socket yet (the
    path exists from mkstemp before fzf replaces it - normal at the start,
    the first ~0.1s), so the caller can retry what still matters."""
    if not sock_path or not os.path.exists(sock_path):
        return False
    conn = http.client.HTTPConnection("localhost", timeout=2)
    try:
        conn.sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX)
        conn.sock.settimeout(2)
        conn.sock.connect(sock_path)
        conn.request("POST", "/", body=action.encode())
        return conn.getresponse().status == 200
    except (OSError, http.client.HTTPException):
        return False
    finally:
        conn.close()


class PickerState:
    """Everything the picker's threads share: the current in-memory
    snapshot, the incrementally-merged session set the reindex child is
    streaming into, and the throttled path from either changing to fzf
    actually redrawing.

    `snapshot` starts as None: fzf is launched before the cache has been
    read (the whole point - the frame is up and typing works immediately),
    and a request that arrives before the fast load has landed waits on
    `ready` rather than answering "nothing" and leaving fzf showing 0/0
    until the next keystroke."""

    def __init__(self, fzf_sock, reload_action):
        self.lock = threading.Lock()
        self.ready = threading.Event()
        self.snapshot = None
        self.sessions = {}      # session_id -> indexed, the merge target
        self.dirty = False      # sessions changed since snapshot was built
        self.label = None       # progress readout wanted ("" clears it)
        self.label_shown = None
        self.fzf_sock = fzf_sock
        self.reload_action = reload_action
        self.wake = threading.Event()

    def get(self):
        self.ready.wait(15)
        return self.snapshot

    def replace(self, snapshot):
        """The fast (cache-only) snapshot, from drive()'s loader thread."""
        with self.lock:
            self.snapshot = snapshot
            self.sessions = dict(snapshot["sessions"])
            self.dirty = False
        self.ready.set()

    def merge(self, entries):
        """A batch of freshly-parsed sessions from the reindex child:
        path -> indexed-or-None. Replaces by session_id (a session that
        changed) or adds (one the cache had never seen); None (an
        empty/unparseable file) contributes nothing to the view."""
        with self.lock:
            for indexed in entries.values():
                if indexed:
                    self.sessions[indexed["session_id"]] = indexed
            self.dirty = True
        self.wake.set()

    def finish(self, session_ids):
        """The reindex child's final word: exactly this set of sessions
        exists on disk now - drop anything the fast snapshot had that has
        since vanished."""
        keep = set(session_ids)
        with self.lock:
            for sid in [sid for sid in self.sessions if sid not in keep]:
                del self.sessions[sid]
            self.dirty = True
            self.label = ""
        self.wake.set()

    def set_label(self, text):
        with self.lock:
            self.label = text
        self.wake.set()

    def refresher(self):
        """Daemon thread: turns merge()/finish()/set_label() calls into fzf
        redraws, at most one list reload per second while frames are
        streaming (each reload re-runs the search for the current query
        and repaints the list - fine a few times, not fine 30 times over
        a 2s reindex) and at most a few label updates a second. The final
        (finish) reload is never held back: `dirty` with an empty label
        means "done", and that one goes out at once.

        The progress readout is the border label, anchored top-left
        (--border-label-pos=2): the top border is the topmost line fzf
        draws, above prompt/list/preview alike, and the left edge is where
        the eye starts. Not its own line inside the frame (an extra header
        line would shift the list and steal a row from resize_on_result's
        budget, which assumes exactly one header line - see the +4 there),
        and not the bottom corner (where a first version put it, and where
        nobody looks). Cleared by posting an empty label once finish()
        lands."""
        last_reload = 0.0
        while True:
            self.wake.wait(0.25)
            self.wake.clear()
            with self.lock:
                label = self.label
                done = self.dirty and self.label == ""
                do_reload = self.dirty and (done or time.time() - last_reload >= 1.0)
                if do_reload:
                    sessions = dict(self.sessions)
                    self.dirty = False
            if do_reload:
                snapshot = _aggregate_snapshot(sessions)
                with self.lock:
                    self.snapshot = snapshot
                self.ready.set()
                last_reload = time.time()
                if not fzf_post(self.fzf_sock, self.reload_action):
                    # fzf not listening yet (or gone): the merged data is
                    # already what the next request serves, but the list
                    # on screen wasn't told - try again next tick
                    with self.lock:
                        self.dirty = True
            if label is not None and label != self.label_shown:
                text = f" ⟳ {label} " if label else ""
                if fzf_post(self.fzf_sock, f"change-border-label({text})"):
                    self.label_shown = label


def _recv_exact(sock, n):
    buf = bytearray()
    while len(buf) < n:
        chunk = sock.recv(n - len(buf))
        if not chunk:
            return None
        buf += chunk
    return bytes(buf)


class PickerServer(socketserver.ThreadingMixIn, socketserver.UnixStreamServer):
    daemon_threads = True
    allow_reuse_address = True


def make_handler(state):
    class Handler(socketserver.BaseRequestHandler):
        def handle(self):
            sock = self.request
            # peek at the mode word: the three client modes send one
            # NUL-separated argv and half-close; the feed mode streams
            # length-prefixed frames until EOF, so it has to be told apart
            # before reading "everything"
            head = b""
            while len(head) < 5:
                head = sock.recv(5, socket.MSG_PEEK)
                if not head or len(head) >= 5 or b"\0" in head:
                    break
                time.sleep(0.001)  # partial first segment - practically never
            if head[:5] == b"feed\0":
                sock.recv(5)
                self.feed(sock)
                return
            data = bytearray()
            while (chunk := sock.recv(1 << 16)):
                data += chunk
            parts = data.decode(errors="replace").split("\0")
            mode, args = parts[0], parts[1:]
            snap = state.get()
            if mode == "search":
                rows = search(snap, args[0] if args else "") if snap else []
                out = "\n".join(rows) + "\n" if rows else ""  # no rows: no
                # bytes at all - a lone "\n" would be one blank list item
            elif mode == "preview":
                out = preview(args[0], args[1] if len(args) > 1 else "", snap)
            elif mode == "complete":
                out = json.dumps(complete_query(args[0] if args else "", snap))
            elif mode == "history":
                out = json.dumps(history_candidates(args[0] if args else ""))
            elif mode == "record":
                # ctrl-j/ctrl-k's execute-silent (drive()'s fzf_argv) -
                # "the moment user finishes typing and makes an action in
                # the data grid... or only start moving selection around",
                # not gated behind a full accept (a real accept is
                # already handled for free by fzf's own --history flag -
                # see HISTORY_PATH). Blind append, no dedup here -
                # dedupe_history() collapses repeats once, after drive()'s
                # own fzf exits.
                q = " ".join((args[0] if args else "").split())
                if q:
                    try:
                        with open(HISTORY_PATH, "a") as hf:
                            hf.write(q + "\n")
                        os.chmod(HISTORY_PATH, 0o600)
                    except OSError:
                        pass
                out = ""
            else:
                out = ""
            try:
                sock.sendall(out.encode())
            except OSError:
                pass  # fzf killed the client (next keystroke) - expected

        def feed(self, sock):
            """One long-lived connection from reindex_and_stream: frames
            of {"type": "sessions"|"done", ...} until EOF."""
            while True:
                hdr = _recv_exact(sock, 4)
                if not hdr:
                    return
                (n,) = struct.unpack("!I", hdr)
                payload = _recv_exact(sock, n)
                if payload is None:
                    return
                try:
                    msg = pickle.loads(payload)
                except Exception:
                    return
                if msg.get("type") == "sessions":
                    state.merge(msg["entries"])
                    pct = int(100 * msg["done"] / max(1, msg["total"]))
                    state.set_label(f"indexing {pct}% - list still filling in")
                elif msg.get("type") == "done":
                    state.finish(msg["session_ids"])
    return Handler


def drive():
    # the one failure worth stopping for up front, before anything is on
    # screen: build_snapshot_fast() would otherwise hit it on the loader
    # thread, where die()'s sys.exit only ends that thread and fzf would
    # sit on an empty list
    if not project_files():
        die(f"no conversation history found matching {PROJECTS_GLOB}")
    sweep_stale_snapshots()
    sdir = snapshot_dir()
    # the picker's own request socket (CLIENT_SRC connects here) and
    # fzf's --listen socket (fzf_post pokes here): both mkstemp'd so
    # sweep_stale_snapshots() has a name+pid to find if this invocation
    # dies before they're removed, then the fd closed immediately - both
    # fzf and UnixStreamServer recreate the path as an actual socket on
    # bind (fzf's docs: "the existing file will be removed"; the server
    # gets an explicit unlink below).
    fd, sock_path = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=f"{SNAP_PREFIX}{os.getpid()}-",
                                     suffix=".sock", dir=sdir)
    os.close(fd)
    atexit.register(lambda: _rm(sock_path))
    fzf_fd, fzf_sock = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=f"{SNAP_PREFIX}{os.getpid()}-fzf-",
                                        suffix=".sock", dir=sdir)
    os.close(fzf_fd)
    atexit.register(lambda: _rm(fzf_sock))
    # `tab`'s completion result lands here (see complete_query) rather
    # than being captured directly - `execute(...)` doesn't feed its own
    # stdout back into fzf, so `transform-query` reads it from this file
    # right after.
    complete_fd, complete_path = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=f"{SNAP_PREFIX}{os.getpid()}-complete-",
                                                   suffix=".txt", dir=sdir)
    os.close(complete_fd)
    atexit.register(lambda: _rm(complete_path))
    history_fd, history_path = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=f"{SNAP_PREFIX}{os.getpid()}-history-",
                                                  suffix=".txt", dir=sdir)
    os.close(history_fd)
    atexit.register(lambda: _rm(history_path))
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(HISTORY_PATH), exist_ok=True)
    client_fd, client_path = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=f"{SNAP_PREFIX}{os.getpid()}-client-",
                                              suffix=".py", dir=sdir)
    with os.fdopen(client_fd, "w") as f:
        f.write(CLIENT_SRC + "\n")
    atexit.register(lambda: _rm(client_path))
    for sig in (signal.SIGHUP, signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGINT):
        signal.signal(sig, lambda *_: sys.exit(1))

    client = shlex.join([sys.executable, "-I", "-S", client_path, sock_path])
    search_cmd = f"{client} search {{q}}"
    preview_cmd = f"{client} preview {{1}} {{q}}"
    complete_cmd = f"{client} complete {{q}} > {shlex.quote(complete_path)}"
    history_cmd = f"{client} history {{q}} > {shlex.quote(history_path)}"
    record_cmd = f"{client} record {{q}}"
    # query-dsl.md "Search-box history": Up/Ctrl+K walk history back, Down/Ctrl+J
    # forward only while a walk is on (else they move the results) - the
    # state lives in ~/bin/fzf-histnav, driven through fzf's `transform`.
    os.environ["HISTNAV_STATE"] = os.path.join(os.environ.get("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR", "/tmp"), f"histnav-{os.getpid()}")
    os.environ["HISTNAV_BIN"] = os.path.expanduser("~/bin/fzf-histnav")
    os.environ["HISTNAV_RECORD"] = record_cmd
    histnav = shlex.quote(os.environ["HISTNAV_BIN"])
    # the reload action re-sends {q} (fzf substitutes it from the CURRENT
    # query box, confirmed directly: an action posted to --listen still
    # gets {q}/{n}-style placeholders resolved against live state, not
    # just ones bound with --bind) so a query the user already started
    # typing survives every refresh instead of being reset to empty.
    state = PickerState(fzf_sock, f"reload({search_cmd})")

    _rm(sock_path)
    server = PickerServer(sock_path, make_handler(state))
    threading.Thread(target=server.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()
    threading.Thread(target=state.refresher, daemon=True).start()

    def load():
        # the fast (cache-only) snapshot - see build_snapshot_fast. Off the
        # main thread so fzf is already on screen, drawn and accepting
        # keystrokes, while this runs; its first `start:reload` request
        # simply waits on state.ready (PickerState.get) for the ~0.1s this
        # takes and then fills the list.
        try:
            snapshot, stale = build_snapshot_fast()
        except Exception as e:  # never leave fzf waiting on `ready`
            _dbg(f"fast load failed: {e!r}")
            state.replace(_aggregate_snapshot({}))
            state.set_label(f" cache load failed: {type(e).__name__} ")
            stale = True
        else:
            state.replace(snapshot)
        if stale:
            # `stale` means the view just served is missing or out of date
            # for at least one session - kick off the real build_snapshot()
            # in a detached child (see reindex_and_stream) that streams
            # each session back over sock_path as it's parsed
            state.set_label("indexing 0% - list still filling in")
            subprocess.Popen(
                [sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__),
                 "--reindex-and-notify", sock_path],
                stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            )  # not waited on - it outlives this invocation on purpose,
            # see reindex_and_stream's docstring; leaving it running after
            # fzf exits is a feature, not a leak, since it's what warms
            # the cache for the *next* prefix+C-c

    threading.Thread(target=load, daemon=True).start()

    resize_on_result = (
        # list gets just enough rows for its matches (+4: the header row,
        # the prompt line, the match-count info line, one extra - $FZF_LINES
        # doesn't shrink to account for --header, confirmed directly: it
        # reported the exact same value with and without one, so the header
        # has to be budgeted for here or it silently steals a row from the
        # list, clipping the last result - this was +3 before the header
        # existed, and +2 before THAT was found one row short the same way),
        # preview gets the rest - EXCEPT the list is also capped at half of
        # FZF_LINES, so the preview never drops below half regardless of
        # match count, and grows past half on its own the fewer results
        # there are to list
        f'hdr={shlex.quote(HEADER_LIVE)}; case "$FZF_QUERY" in *"/fts/"*arch*) hdr={shlex.quote(HEADER_ARCHIVE)};; esac; '
        'case "$FZF_QUERY" in *"/fts"*) ;; *) echo "change-header($hdr)+hide-preview"; exit;; esac; '
        'c=$FZF_MATCH_COUNT; list_rows=$((c + 4)); '
        '[ "$list_rows" -lt 4 ] && list_rows=4; '
        'half=$((FZF_LINES / 2)); '
        '[ "$list_rows" -gt "$half" ] && list_rows=$half; '
        'echo "change-header($hdr)+show-preview+change-preview-window(down,$((FZF_LINES - list_rows)))"'
    )
    fzf_argv = [
        # fzf's default full-screen mode already takes over the terminal
        # via the alternate screen buffer and restores it on exit - the
        # "popup" here, no tmux display-popup needed (see module
        # docstring for why prefix+C-c avoids one: popups don't resize
        # live and don't set $TMUX_PANE). --border/--margin just gives it
        # a visually inset look rather than filling every last cell.
        "fzf", "--ansi", "--disabled", "--layout=reverse",
        "--border=rounded", "--margin=2,4",
        # the "indexing N%" progress readout (PickerState.refresher) sits
        # on the top border, left-anchored - the topmost line fzf draws
        "--border-label-pos=2", "--color=label:bold:yellow",
        "--delimiter", "\t", "--with-nth", "2..",
        "--prompt", "",
        "--header", HEADER_LIVE,
        "--preview", preview_cmd,
        "--preview-window", "down,50%,border-top,wrap,hidden",
        "--bind", f"start:reload:{search_cmd}",
        "--bind", f"change:reload({search_cmd})",
        "--bind", f"result:transform:{resize_on_result}",
        # `result` alone only re-runs this on a filtering change - a
        # terminal resize while sitting on an unchanged query never fired
        # it, so the list/preview split stayed exactly as computed at
        # whatever size the window happened to be when you last typed.
        # `resize` (fzf 0.44+) is the event fzf itself provides for this,
        # triggered on terminal size change specifically - same
        # transform, just a second trigger for it.
        "--bind", f"resize:transform:{resize_on_result}",
        "--bind", "ctrl-left:backward-word",
        "--bind", "ctrl-right:forward-word",
        # Search-box history (query-dsl.md): Up/Down cycle submitted
        # queries most-recent-first (fzf's own --history below, rebound
        # here from its up/down list-navigation default) with
        # draft-restore; row navigation moves to ctrl-j/ctrl-k instead
        # (already fzf's default down/up synonyms), which also record the
        # current query into history the moment selection actually moves
        # - not just on a full accept (that's --history's own job) - see
        # history_candidates()'s "record" mode.
        "--bind", f"up:transform({histnav} up)",
        "--bind", f"ctrl-k:transform({histnav} up)",
        "--bind", f"down:transform({histnav} down)",
        "--bind", f"ctrl-j:transform({histnav} down)",
        # `execute` hands the terminal over to complete_cmd (same as
        # fzf's own `execute(less {})` example) - a genuine nested
        # interactive picker when there's more than one candidate, see
        # complete_query/CLIENT_SRC - then `transform-query` loads
        # whatever it wrote back into the search box, which also
        # re-triggers `change` (a query change always does) so results
        # refresh against the completed query.
        "--bind", f"tab:execute({complete_cmd})+transform-query(cat {shlex.quote(complete_path)})",
        # ctrl-space also opens completion, same as tab (query-dsl.md
        # Autocompletion section - ClipboardPicker.qml/NotificationPicker.qml's
        # reference implementation, ported here). Only meaningful at this
        # outer level, where no popup is open yet to AND-narrow - the nested
        # fzf spawned by complete_cmd already binds its own ctrl-space to
        # put( ) for that (see the "complete> " fzf above).
        "--bind", f"ctrl-space:execute({complete_cmd})+transform-query(cat {shlex.quote(complete_path)})",
        # Search-box history (query-dsl.md): ctrl-p/ctrl-n cycle
        # submitted queries via fzf's own --history below; ctrl-r opens
        # the fuzzy history popup, same execute+transform-query split as
        # tab above, just pointed at history_candidates/CLIENT_SRC.
        "--bind", f"ctrl-r:execute({history_cmd})+transform-query(cat {shlex.quote(history_path)})",
        "--history", HISTORY_PATH,
        f"--listen={fzf_sock}",
    ]
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(fzf_argv, capture_output=True, text=True)
    finally:
        server.shutdown()
        server.server_close()
        _rm(sock_path)
        _rm(fzf_sock)
        _rm(complete_path)
        _rm(history_path)
        _rm(client_path)
        dedupe_history()

    selected = result.stdout.strip()
    if not selected:
        return
    session_id = selected.split("\t", 1)[0]
    if session_id.startswith("tmux:"):          # a scrollback hit: go to that pane
        client = current_client_tty()
        if client:
            jump_to_pane(client, session_id[5:])
        return
    # the latest view, not the one fzf started with: the session actually
    # selected may only exist in what the reindex child streamed in later
    with state.lock:
        s = state.sessions.get(session_id)
    if not s:
        die(f"selected session {session_id} vanished from the snapshot")
    resolve(session_id, s["cwd"])


FTS_CONF = os.path.expanduser("~/.cache/indexes/claude/recoll")


def fts_terms(text):
    """This DSL's words as a Recoll query: `!word` -> `-word` (same mapping as
    the search server's /fts for every other content index)."""
    toks = ["-" + t[1:] if t.startswith("!") and len(t) > 1 else t
            for t in shlex.split(text.replace("\n", " "), posix=False)]
    # live typing: the last bare word is still being typed, so it matches as a prefix
    if toks and toks[-1].isalnum():
        toks[-1] += "*"
    return " ".join(toks)


TMUX_ROOT = os.path.expanduser("~/.cache/indexes/tmux")
TMUX_CONF = os.path.join(TMUX_ROOT, "recoll")


def fts_search(text, conf=None):
    """[(session_id, relevance %)] best first, from the Recoll index of Claude
    conversations (~/.cache/indexes/claude, kept by claude-fts-index). The one
    full-text engine behind alt-tab's and the usage panel's /fts and
    this tool's own --fts."""
    terms = fts_terms(text)
    if not terms.strip():
        return []
    r = subprocess.run(["recollq", "-c", conf or FTS_CONF, "-n", "0-5000", "-F", "url relevancyrating", "--", terms],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    out = []
    for line in r.stdout.splitlines():
        parts = line.split()
        if len(parts) != 2:
            continue
        try:
            url, rating = (base64.b64decode(p).decode("utf-8", "replace") for p in parts)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        out.append((os.path.basename(url)[:-4], float(rating.rstrip("%") or 0)))
    return out


def tmux_rows(text, limit=300):
    """tmux scrollback hits (the Recoll index claude-fts-index keeps of every non-Claude pane), best
    first, as rows shaped like session_rows' (kind "tmux", id "tmux:%12"); only panes that still exist."""
    try:
        meta = json.load(open(os.path.join(TMUX_ROOT, "meta.json")))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return []
    r = subprocess.run(["tmux", "list-panes", "-a", "-F", "#{pane_id}"], capture_output=True, text=True)
    alive = set(r.stdout.split())
    hits = fts_search(text, TMUX_CONF) if text.strip() else \
        [(d, 0.0) for d, m in sorted(meta.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1].get("mtime", 0))]
    rows = []
    for doc, score in hits:
        m = meta.get(doc)
        if not m or m["pane_id"] not in alive:
            continue
        rows.append({"session_id": "tmux:" + m["pane_id"], "kind": "tmux", "title": m["title"] or m["command"],
                     "cwd": m["cwd"], "msgs": "", "tokens": None, "mtime": m["mtime"], "account": "",
                     "profile": "", "score": score, "live": False, "status": "tmux",
                     "sess": m["session_id"].lstrip("$"), "win": m["window_id"].lstrip("@"),
                     "pane": m["pane_id"].lstrip("%"), "pane_id": m["pane_id"], "session_name": m["session"],
                     "window_name": m["window_name"], "command": m["command"]})
        if len(rows) >= limit:
            break
    return rows


def fts_snippets(sid, text, limit=6, ctx=90):
    """Lines of context around where `text`'s words occur in conversation
    `sid` (read from the Recoll collection's text copy); hits are wrapped in
    \\x01..\\x02 for the caller to style. Shared by alt-tab and the usage
    panel's match preview."""
    if sid.startswith("tmux:"):      # a tmux pane's scrollback copy, see claude-fts-index
        path = os.path.join(TMUX_ROOT, "panes", "p" + sid[5:].lstrip("%") + ".txt")
    else:
        path = os.path.expanduser(f"~/.cache/indexes/claude/sessions/{sid}.txt")
    try:
        body = open(path, errors="replace").read()
    except OSError:
        return []
    words = [w.strip("*") for w in re.findall(r"[^\s\"!]+", text) if not w.startswith("-")]
    words = [w for w in words if w]
    if not words:
        return []
    rx = re.compile("|".join(re.escape(w) for w in sorted(words, key=len, reverse=True)), re.I)
    out, last_end = [], -1
    for m in rx.finditer(body):
        if m.start() < last_end:
            continue
        a, b = max(0, m.start() - ctx), min(len(body), m.end() + ctx)
        chunk = body[a:b]
        out.append(("… " if a else "") + rx.sub(lambda h: "\x01" + h.group() + "\x02", " ".join(chunk.split())) + (" …" if b < len(body) else ""))
        last_end = b
        if len(out) >= limit:
            break
    return out


def session_rows(text, scope="archive", limit=300):
    """Conversations (and tmux scrollback panes) for the table views: the ones matching `text` best
    first, or the newest when `text` is empty. `scope` is a comma list of live / archive / tmux ("all" =
    live,archive). Claude rows come from the indexer's meta.json; whether one is running is decided here,
    at query time, from the sessions registry - the index itself doesn't know. Each row carries `live`."""
    wanted = set()
    for part in scope.split(","):
        wanted |= {"live", "archive"} if part == "all" else {part}
    rows = []
    if wanted & {"live", "archive"}:
        try:
            meta = json.load(open(os.path.expanduser("~/.cache/indexes/claude/meta.json")))
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            meta = {}
        live_map = get_live_sessions()
        live = set(live_map)
        want = lambda sid: (sid in live and "live" in wanted) or (sid not in live and "archive" in wanted)
        if text.strip():
            order = [(sid, score) for sid, score in fts_search(text) if sid in meta and want(sid)]
        else:
            order = [(sid, 0.0) for sid, m in sorted(meta.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1].get("mtime", 0))
                     if want(sid)]
        profiles = profile_map()
        for sid, score in order[:limit]:
            m = meta[sid]
            prof = profiles.get(sid)
            rows.append({"session_id": sid, "kind": "claude", "title": m.get("title") or "", "cwd": m.get("cwd") or "",
                         "msgs": m.get("msgs") or 0, "tokens": m.get("tokens"), "mtime": m.get("mtime"),
                         "account": account_name(prof), "profile": prof or "", "score": score,
                         "live": sid in live, "status": (live_map.get(sid) or {}).get("status", "")})
    if "tmux" in wanted:
        rows += tmux_rows(text, limit)
    if text.strip():
        rows.sort(key=lambda r: -r["score"])
    return rows[:limit]


def resume_cmd(sid):
    """(cwd, env, argv) to resume `sid` under the account it last ran as."""
    try:
        meta = json.load(open(os.path.expanduser("~/.cache/indexes/claude/meta.json"))).get(sid) or {}
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        meta = {}
    cwd = meta.get("cwd") or HOME
    if not os.path.isdir(cwd):
        cwd = HOME
    profile = find_session_profile(sid)
    env = dict(os.environ)
    if profile:
        env["CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"] = profile
    return cwd, env, profile, ["claude", "--resume", sid, "--dangerously-skip-permissions"]


def focus_tmux_pane(pane_id):
    """Go to tmux pane `pane_id` ("%12") and focus the Hyprland window showing its session: select the
    window and pane, find the client attached to that session, walk its process ancestry to a window
    Hyprland knows, focus it. No such window: open a terminal attached to the session."""
    r = subprocess.run(["tmux", "display-message", "-p", "-t", pane_id, "#{session_id}\t#{window_id}"],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    if r.returncode or "\t" not in r.stdout:
        return
    sess, win = r.stdout.strip().split("\t")
    subprocess.run(["tmux", "select-window", "-t", win])
    subprocess.run(["tmux", "select-pane", "-t", pane_id])
    clients = subprocess.run(["tmux", "list-clients", "-F", "#{client_pid}\t#{session_id}"],
                             capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.splitlines()
    pids = [int(l.split("\t")[0]) for l in clients if l.split("\t")[-1] == sess and l.split("\t")[0].isdigit()]
    wins = {}
    try:
        for c in json.loads(subprocess.run(["hyprctl", "-j", "clients"], capture_output=True, text=True).stdout):
            wins[c["pid"]] = c["address"]
    except (ValueError, OSError):
        pass
    for pid in pids:
        cur = pid
        while cur > 1:
            if cur in wins:
                script = ("for _, w in ipairs(hl.get_windows({})) do if tostring(w.address) == \"%s\" then "
                          "hl.dispatch(hl.dsp.focus({ window = w })) break end end" % wins[cur])
                subprocess.run(["hyprctl", "repl", script])
                return
            try:
                cur = int(open(f"/proc/{cur}/stat").read().rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()[1])
            except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
                break
    subprocess.Popen(["alacritty", "-e", "tmux", "attach-session", "-t", sess], stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                     stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, start_new_session=True)


def resume_window(sid):
    """Resume `sid` in its own new tmux session, shown in a new alacritty window
    (Hyprland), for alt-tab / the usage panel."""
    cwd, _, profile, argv = resume_cmd(sid)
    cmd = ["tmux", "new-session", "-d", "-P", "-F", "#{session_id} #{pane_id}", "-c", cwd]
    if profile:
        cmd += ["-e", f"CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR={profile}"]
    r = subprocess.run(cmd + [shlex.join(argv)], capture_output=True, text=True)
    parts = r.stdout.split()
    if len(parts) != 2:
        print(f"claude-history: tmux new-session failed: {r.stderr.strip()}", file=sys.stderr)
        return
    target, pane = parts
    label_window(profile, pane)
    subprocess.Popen(["alacritty", "-e", "tmux", "attach-session", "-t", target],
                     stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                     start_new_session=True)


def resume_tmux(sid):
    """Resume `sid` in a new tmux window, labelled with its account."""
    cwd, _, profile, argv = resume_cmd(sid)
    cmd = ["tmux", "new-window", "-P", "-F", "#{pane_id}", "-c", cwd]
    if profile:
        cmd += ["-e", f"CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR={profile}"]
    r = subprocess.run(cmd + [shlex.join(argv)], capture_output=True, text=True)
    pane = r.stdout.strip()
    if pane:
        label_window(profile, pane)


def fts_client(text):
    for sid, score in fts_search(text):
        print(f"{sid}\t{score:.0f}")


def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
    parser.add_argument("--snippets", nargs=2, metavar=("SESSION", "QUERY"),
                        help="print match excerpts of QUERY in SESSION (hits wrapped in \\x01 \\x02)")
    parser.add_argument("--sessions", metavar="QUERY", nargs="?", const="",
                        help="JSON list of conversations matching QUERY (best first, or newest), for the table views")
    parser.add_argument("--scope", default="archive",
                        help="with --sessions: comma list of live, archive, tmux (all = live,archive); default archive")
    parser.add_argument("--focus-tmux", metavar="PANE", help="select tmux PANE (%%12) and focus the window showing its session")
    parser.add_argument("--resume-window", metavar="SESSION", help="resume SESSION in a new alacritty window")
    parser.add_argument("--resume-tmux", metavar="SESSION", help="resume SESSION in a new tmux window")
    parser.add_argument("--fts", metavar="QUERY",
                        help="print session_id<TAB>score for QUERY, best first")
    parser.add_argument("--reindex-and-notify", metavar="SOCKET",
                        help="internal: reparse changed transcripts, stream them to the picker at SOCKET")
    args = parser.parse_args()

    if args.sessions is not None:
        print(json.dumps(session_rows(args.sessions, args.scope)))
    elif args.focus_tmux:
        focus_tmux_pane(args.focus_tmux)
    elif args.resume_window:
        resume_window(args.resume_window)
    elif args.resume_tmux:
        resume_tmux(args.resume_tmux)
    elif args.snippets:
        print("\n".join(fts_snippets(*args.snippets)))
    elif args.fts is not None:
        fts_client(args.fts)
    elif args.reindex_and_notify:
        reindex_and_stream(args.reindex_and_notify)
    else:
        drive()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
